lesson_number: 3
title: Context Engineering
topic_summary:
  "Plan the lesson as the bridge from basic prompt engineering to production-grade agent systems, showing why
  single-call optimization fails for stateful applications involving memory, tools, history, and dynamic data needs. Introduce
  context engineering as the discipline of dynamically selecting, assembling, compressing, and formatting information from
  short-term and long-term memory to optimize LLM performance, cost, and latency. Contrast it explicitly with prompt engineering
  (subset, stateless) and fine-tuning (expensive, static). Outline memory taxonomy (procedural/episodic/semantic, short-term
  working memory components), core production failure modes, and four families of optimization strategies, ending with a concrete
  healthcare walkthrough and the broader SWE/data/Ops skill stack required.

  "
why_valuable:
  "As AI engineers move from simple chatbots and RAG to memory-enabled, tool-using agents, the ability to precisely
  manage what enters the context window becomes the dominant factor in accuracy, speed, cost, and scalability — far more practical
  than repeated fine-tuning for most enterprise use cases. Mastering context engineering teaches systems thinking that combines
  prompt craft, retrieval, state management, and observability, preparing engineers to build reliable production AI instead
  of brittle demos.

  "
target_length_words: 3300
theory_practice_ratio: 65% theory - 35% practice
lesson_scope: > # TODO: review
  Lesson 3 in a progressive course; follows the prior lesson on choosing between
  LLM workflows vs. full agents, and precedes dedicated lessons on structured
  outputs, multi-agent patterns (orchestrator-worker), tool/action design,
  memory implementations, and RAG.
audience:
  "AI engineers and developers who already understand LLM basics, prompt engineering patterns, and have experimented
  with simple agents or RAG, but need a systematic framework for managing growing context, state, and cost in production systems.

  "
concepts_from_previous_lessons:
  - Choosing between LLM workflows and full AI agents for different system designs
concepts_for_future_lessons:
  - Structured outputs and schemas to reduce ambiguity in downstream steps
  - Orchestrator-worker multi-agent patterns for isolating context
  - Implementing and retrieving from different memory types (episodic, semantic, procedural)
  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) techniques and tradeoffs
  - Defining, evaluating, and limiting the number of available tools/actions
  - Observability, tracing, and evaluation of context quality in production
sections:
  - title: Introduction -- When Prompt Engineering Breaks
    target_words: 220
    key_points:
      - Short evolution arc of AI applications from simple chatbots (2022) through RAG (2023)
        and tool agents (2024) to memory-enabled agents (2025)
      - Callback to core concepts from previous lessons as the entry point
      - "Transition frame: as agents grew more stateful and complex, managing what enters
        the context window became the central engineering challenge"
  - title: From Prompt to Context Engineering
    target_words: 275
    key_points:
      - "Core limitation of prompt engineering: stateless, single-interaction focus fails as
        conversation history, user data, and tool schemas accumulate"
      - "Context decay: noise from ever-growing context causes the model to lose original
        instructions and hallucinate"
      - "Real-world cost/latency example: why stuffing everything into a large context window
        backfires in practice"
      - "Shift in mindset: from static prompt writing to dynamic systems that select critical
        context per LLM call"
      - "Context engineering as the answer: treat AI apps as systems that dynamically select
        minimal sufficient context from memory, databases, and tools"
  - title: Understanding Context Engineering
    target_words: 500
    key_points:
      - "OS/RAM analogy: frame the context window as the LLM's limited working memory that
        must be actively managed"
      - "Context engineering as an optimization problem: retrieve the minimal sufficient
        subset from short/long-term memory for the current task"
      - "Relationship to prompt engineering: context engineering decides what to provide;
        prompt engineering decides how to phrase it -- show as a diagram"
      - "Comparison table: prompt engineering vs context engineering across scope, state,
        and focus"
      - "Decision criteria for choosing context engineering over fine-tuning: dynamic data,
        iteration speed, and cost"
      - "Enterprise reality: strong reasoning models + well-engineered context solve most
        production problems without retraining"
  - title: What Makes Up the Context
    target_words: 500
    key_points:
      - Context = everything dynamically assembled before each LLM call, not a static artifact
      - "Assembly workflow diagram: user input triggers memory pulls -- prompt template --
        LLM call -- memory update"
      - "Short-term working memory components: user input, message history, agent reasoning
        and thoughts, tool calls and their outputs"
      - "Long-term memory taxonomy with human memory parallels: procedural (instructions/schemas),
        episodic (user history), semantic (external knowledge/docs)"
      - All components are recomputed dynamically per turn; context engineering is the skill
        of selecting the right subset each time
      - Forward-link to future lessons (structured outputs, tools, memory, RAG) while keeping
        current lesson at intuitive level
  - title: Production Implementation Challenges
    target_words: 400
    key_points:
      - "Overarching goal: keep context as small as possible while still giving the LLM enough
        signal"
      - Context window hard limit and why larger windows do not eliminate the fundamental problem
      - "Lost-in-the-middle: model performance degrades well before the hard token limit;
        middle content is effectively ignored"
      - "Context drift: conflicting facts accumulate over time without a resolution mechanism"
      - "Tool confusion: too many or poorly described actions degrade tool selection accuracy"
  - title: Key Strategies for Context Optimization
    target_words: 650
    key_points:
      - "Right-context selection strategies: structured outputs, targeted retrieval, reduced
        tool sets, and instruction placement at prompt edges"
      - Evidence and heuristics for tool count vs accuracy; evaluate on business metrics
        rather than generic benchmarks
      - "Context compression: summarize history, extract preferences to long-term episodic
        memory, deduplicate"
      - "Context isolation: split context across specialized agents instead of one massive
        shared context (orchestrator-worker pattern)"
      - "Format optimizations: XML-style semantic tagging, YAML over JSON for token efficiency"
      - "Observability imperative: inspect the exact context sent to the LLM via traces --
        the key leap from prototype to production"
  - title: Practical Example
    target_words: 500
    key_points:
      - Real-world scenarios illustrating different context needs across industries (healthcare,
        finance, project management, content creation)
      - "Healthcare assistant walkthrough: dynamically retrieve patient episodic memory and
        relevant medical literature for a personalized response"
      - "Code pattern: assemble prompt with XML semantic delimiters and structured data
        representations"
      - "Production stack preview: Gemini, LangGraph, vector/graph databases, Opik/LangSmith
        for observability"
  - title: Conclusion -- Connecting Context Engineering to AI Engineering
    target_words: 275
    key_points:
      - "Context engineering as art and science: intuition for minimal sufficient information
        rather than isolated prompt tricks"
      - "Interdisciplinary nature: AI engineering + software engineering + data engineering
        + operations"
      - "Systems thinking shift: from building individual components to architecting scalable,
        observable, maintainable AI products"
      - Course philosophy -- teach the combination of skills needed to move beyond demos to
        production-ready agents
